In this lesson we'll look at the main logarithm laws and how to switch between different bases, which will make solving log problems way easier. First up, the product law. Multiplying two numbers inside a log is the same as adding their logs together. So the log of m times n equals the log of m plus the log of n. Next, the quotient law does the opposite for division. The log of m divided by n equals the log of m minus the log of n. Then there's the power law, which is a real time saver. If you have something raised to a power inside a log, you can bring that power out to the front and multiply. So the log of m to the n equals n times the log of m. Finally, the change of base formula lets you convert a log in any base into one your calculator can handle, usually base ten or the natural log. You just divide the log of the top by the log of the bottom, keeping the base the same on both. Now let's see it in action.